.TH REGEXP 6
.SH NAME
regexp \- regular expression notation
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.I "regular expression"
specifies a set of strings.
A member of that set is
.I matched
by the expression.
This is the notation
.IR grep (1)
and
.IR sed (1)
share, through
.IR regexp (2).
The word "character" below means any byte but newline.
.PP
The syntax for a regular expression
.B e0
is
.IP
.EX
e3:  literal | class | '.' | '^' | '$' | '(' e0 ')'

e2:  e3
  |  e2 REP

REP: '*' | '+' | '?'

e1:  e2
  |  e1 e2

e0:  e1
  |  e0 '|' e1
.EE
.PP
A
.B literal
is any character that is not a metacharacter, one of
.BR .*+?[]()|\e^$ ,
or a metacharacter preceded by
.BR \e .
.PP
A
.B class
is a nonempty string
.I s
bracketed
.BI [ s ]
(or
.BI [^ s ]
for its complement), matching any character in (or not in)
.IR s .
A negated class never matches newline.
A substring
.IB a - b ,
with
.I a
and
.I b
in ascending order, stands for the inclusive range of characters between
.I a
and
.IR b .
Inside
.IR s ,
the metacharacters
.B \-
and
.B ]
and an initial
.B ^
must be preceded by
.BR \e ;
other metacharacters have no special meaning there.
.PP
A
.B .
matches any character but newline.
A
.B ^
matches the beginning of the text or the character after a newline.
A
.B $
matches the end of the text or the character before a newline.
.PP
The
.B REP
operators match zero or more
.RB ( * ),
one or more
.RB ( + )
or zero or one
.RB ( ? )
instances of the preceding expression
.BR e2 .
.PP
A concatenation
.B e1 e2
matches a match to
.B e1
followed by a match to
.BR e2 .
An alternation
.B e0|e1
matches either a match to
.B e0
or a match to
.BR e1 .
Parentheses group a subexpression without capturing it: there is no way
in this dialect to refer back to what a group matched.
.PP
A match to any part of a pattern extends as far as possible without
preventing a match to the remainder of the pattern, and among matches
that begin at the same point, the longest is the one found.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR grep (1),
.IR sed (1),
.IR regexp (2)
